%I A399783 #31 Sep 21 2026 15:14:11
%S A399783 2,1,0,0,0,0,0,0,2,26,237,475,2433,6155,43296,160159,988450,3500484,
%T A399783 11984532,35421318,55225956,115384640,137436867,204058884
%N A399783 Power chains: Number of essentially different linear (non-circular) permutations of the numbers 1 to n such that the sum of adjacent numbers is a perfect power, while the sum of the first and last numbers is not a perfect power.
%C A399783 The reversals of a permutation are not counted as different.
%C A399783 The sum of the first and last numbers is required not to be a perfect power, thus the chain cannot be closed to form a power loop (circular permutation), as counted by A399784.
%C A399783 The first linear power chain occurs for n = 7, whereas the first power loop occurs for n = 17 (A399784).
%C A399783 Note that "linear", "non-circular" and "in a row" describe the same type of permutation.
%C A399783 _Giovanni Resta_ found that the least integer n such that the numbers 1 through n can be arranged in a row so that the sum of every two adjacent numbers is a cube is n = 305 (see Carlos Rivera link). For squares, the least such n is 15 (A398909), while for perfect powers it is 7.
%C A399783 This sequence, together with A399784, A399785 and A399786, gives the four possible distinct counts of power chains (see Crossrefs).
%C A399783 In terms of graph theory, these numbers count Hamiltonian paths whose two endpoints are not adjacent, with reversals not counted as distinct (see the solution in the Diophante link to visualize examples of such Hamiltonian paths). Hence such paths cannot be closed to form a Hamiltonian cycle.
%H A399783 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A399783/a399783_1.gp.txt">PARI program</a>.
%H A399783 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/h-graphes-et-circuits/681-h103-les-chaines-de-puissances">H103. Les chaînes de puissances</a> (in French).
%H A399783 Carlos Rivera, <a href="https://www.primepuzzles.net/puzzles/puzz_311.htm">Puzzle 311: Sum to a cube</a>, The Prime Puzzles and Problems Connection.
%F A399783 a(n) = A399785(n) - A399784(n).
%F A399783 a(n) = A399786(n) - n*A399784(n).
%e A399783 a(8) = 1. The only possible linear power chain of length 8 is [4, 5, 3, 6, 2, 7, 1, 8]. Its endpoints satisfy 4 + 8 = 12, which is not a perfect power.
%o A399783 (PARI) \\ See Corneth link
%Y A399783 The four possible distinct counts of power chains: this sequence (number of linear permutations), A399784 (number of circular permutations), A399785 (number of total permutations counting each circular solution only once), A399786 (number of total permutations counting each circular solution n times).
%Y A399783 Cf. A001597 (perfect powers), A398909 (similar, but with square chains).
%K A399783 nonn,more,new
%O A399783 7,1
